${\mathrm{Cu}}_{3}{\big(}{\mathrm{BTC}}{\big)}_{2},$ (1,3,5 benzenetricarboxylic acid, BTC) or HKUST-1 (herein referred to as Cu-BTC), has previously been investigated for numerous applications, including ammonia removal.12 The material was found to have an exceptionally high ammonia loading, but at the expense of framework stability. Due to a combination of ammonia and moisture, the structure essentially was shown to completely disintegrate. Further studies on Cu-BTC by Gul-E-Noor et al. using $\mathrm{^{1}\bar{H}}$ and $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ solid-state NMR indicated that moisture itself is capable of degrading the MOF structure, rendering it ineffective for use in a multitude of applications.11
